  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 ...High pressure builds offshore of New England \ntonight ahead of an approaching frontal system that will move \nacross the area Sunday and Sunday night.\n\nOne more night of fair weather tonight as high pressure builds \noffshore of New England tonight ahead of an approaching frontal \nsystem. After dewpoints drop during the day today, they will creep \nup again tonight into Sunday morning.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Rainy conditions Sunday into Sunday night with \nheavy rainfall possible.\n\nA warm front will lift into the region during the day Sunday, but \nlikely not make it past central/southern portions of Delmarva and \nsouthern New Jersey, before a cold front and triple point low \napproach the area late in the overnight. We expect a couple of \nrounds of precipitation as these systems move into the area. The \nfirst will begin late morning into the afternoon hours as a short \nwave/vorticity impulse approaches from the west. This round will \nlikely be mostly showers with no thunder activity as we will be \nnorth of the warm front and instability will be minimal to non \nexistent. Rainfall with this first batch will likely be mostly 0.25-\n0.50 inches or less area-wide.\n\nThe next batch is expected to move into the area during the evening \nand overnight hours as the second short wave/vorticity impulse moves \ninto the area ahead of the approaching cold front and possible \ntriple point low. PWATS continue to increase to 2-2.25 inches, so \nthere will remain the threat for heavy rainfall as this rainfall \nmoves across the area. It looks like the heavy rainfall will be \nfairly progressive, so we are not planning on a Flash Flood Watch at \nthis time. However, there will may still be some localized flash \nflooding if any training does occur, especially in urbanized and \npoor drainage areas as 1-2 inches of rain, with locally higher \namounts, will be possible overnight\n\nThere is still not a lot of instability since we'll still be north \nof the warm front, so thunderstorms are not expected to be \nwidespread. They still can't be ruled out however, and any severe \nthreat looks to be across portions of southern New Jersey and \ncentral/southern portions of Delmarva which will be closer to the \nwarm front. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...A trailing mid level short wave trough and weak cold \nfront may result in another round of showers and thunderstorms \nMonday Afternoon and Evening.\n\nThe cold front associated with the low that approaches the region on \nSunday will finally progress through the region and move offshore\non Monday. Additionally, in the the mid-levels, a trailing \nshort wave trough will cross overhead. The net result is that \nisolated to scattered showers and thunderstorms will be possible\nduring the day on Monday. \n\nAs for hazards, this round appears to have very limited threats for \nboth flash flooding and severe hazards. Convection on Monday will be \nmuch more weakly forced (as compared to Sunday/Sunday Night), and \nbulk shear values are depicted in the 20 to 25 kt range, which is \nrather modest. This limits the severe threat. Model soundings depict \nstill relatively high precipitable water values, and warm cloud \nlayers above 10K ft, which results in some heavy rain threat. \nHowever, with limited shear and coverage of convection, the threat \nfor any one area to get heavy rain for long enough to result in \nflash flooding remains low.\n\nFor those hoping for a significant cool down in the wake of this \ncold front, unfortunately the temperature gradient is very small. As \nsuch highs on Tuesday will be at or just a degree or two lower than \nMonday. That being said, the front should usher in drier air, so \nregardless it should feel more comfortable on Tuesday. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 4...A cold front is expected to cross through the region \nThursday into Friday.\n\nThe next main focus is another cold front arriving in the region \nduring the Thursday into Friday time period. There are some big \ntiming differences between models on when exactly the front will \narrive and when the mid level trough will cross offshore. As \nsuch, there is considerable uncertainty with the potential \nhazards as the severe and hydro threats will be dependent on \nthe prime time for any showers and thunderstorms.\n\nIn terms of temperatures, so far this appears to be a stronger cold \nfront than the early week cold front. As such, temperatures going \ninto the weekend should be around 5 degrees lower compared to \nthe midweek time period.",
